---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RotateTool from '../components/RotateTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Rotate or Flip an Image Online | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Turn a sideways photo upright or mirror it in your browser, with no upload. Fix a picture that looks rotated on a form, then resize it to the KB limit you need.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: rotate or flip an image', '/rotate-image/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/rotate-image/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Rotate or flip an image" lead="Add a JPEG, PNG or WebP picture, turn it a quarter at a time or mirror it, check the preview and download it. Nothing is uploaded." />

  <RotateTool client:load />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool turns images inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript
      and reload to use it. The explanation below of why pictures end up sideways still applies if you prefer another
      editor.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Why a picture can look sideways on a form</h2>
    <p>
      A phone camera records pixels in the orientation of its sensor, which is usually landscape. When you hold the
      phone upright, it adds a small label to the file saying "show this one turned a quarter". Most photo apps and web
      browsers obey that label, so the picture looks upright to you. Some upload forms, older software and image
      processors ignore it and show the raw pixels, so the same photo appears on its side.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cure is to save the picture with its pixels already turned the right way. That is what this page does: it
      draws the picture the way your browser shows it, turns it and saves the result, so the orientation no longer
      depends on a label that another program may skip.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use the buttons</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Add the picture.</strong> Drag it onto the box, or choose it from your phone or computer.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the preview.</strong> It shows the picture as your browser displays it, with the orientation label already applied once.</li>
      <li><strong>Turn or mirror.</strong> Rotate left or right moves it a quarter turn each time. Flip horizontally mirrors left to right, and flip vertically mirrors top to bottom.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset if you overshoot.</strong> Reset returns to the picture as first shown.</li>
      <li><strong>Download.</strong> The file keeps its format: a JPEG stays a JPEG, a PNG stays a PNG and a WebP stays a WebP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What happens to the quality</h2>
    <p>
      Turning a PNG loses nothing, because PNG stores every pixel exactly. A JPEG or WebP picture has to be compressed
      again when it is saved, and this page uses a high quality setting so the change is small. Even so, each extra
      save of a JPEG removes a little detail, so avoid rotating the same file many times. If you only need the
      orientation fixed once, do it once, from the original.
    </p>
    <p>
      The saved file can be larger or smaller than the one you started with, because a fresh save is not identical to
      the camera's own. If a form also sets a size limit, the quickest route is to rotate first and then reduce the file
      with the <a href="/">resizer on the home page</a>, which can aim for the KB number your form states.
    </p>

    <h2>Mirror images and selfies</h2>
    <p>
      Front cameras often show a mirrored preview, and some apps save that mirror image. Text on a shirt or a sign then
      reads backwards. Flip horizontally to correct it. If you want a mirrored copy on purpose, for a design or a
      reflection effect, the same button does that.
    </p>

    <h2>What this page does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It turns in quarter steps only. It does not rotate by a free angle or straighten a tilted horizon.</li>
      <li>It does not crop, resize or change the file size on purpose.</li>
      <li>It handles one picture at a time.</li>
      <li>Very large pictures above about 16 megapixels are refused, because some phones cannot draw a canvas that big. Reduce them first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a form that asks for a specific size in kilobytes, see the <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">20 KB page</a>{' '}
      or the <a href="/resize-image-to-100kb/">100 KB page</a> after turning your picture.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about rotating images</h2>
    <h3>Why does my photo look upright on my phone but sideways on the website?</h3>
    <p>
      Your phone obeys the orientation label stored in the file. The website ignores it. Saving the picture with its
      pixels turned, as this page does, fixes the difference.
    </p>
    <h3>Will rotating change the file size?</h3>
    <p>
      It can. A JPEG or WebP is saved again, so the size may move a little up or down. Use the resizer afterwards if you
      have a limit in KB.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it remove the location stored in my photo?</h3>
    <p>
      Saving a new copy from the canvas does not carry the original file's extra data across, which usually includes
      location. Do not rely on that as a privacy tool, and check the file yourself if it matters.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I rotate a picture by 45 degrees?</h3>
    <p>
      No. This page turns by 90 degrees at a time. A free angle needs a full photo editor.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my picture uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The turning happens in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
